              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"} Thom spectrum
          %p
            Now for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo =
                  %mi Fr
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} = Fr
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing"} framing
            structure, then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi Fr
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M Fr \simeq \mathbb{S}
          %p
            is equivalently the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom%27s+theorem"} Thom's theorem
            states that there is an isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                    %mi fr
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^{fr}_\bullet
                  \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\pi_\bullet(\mathbb{S})
          %p between the framed cobordism ring and the stable homotopy groups of spheres.
          %p
            For discussion of computation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\bullet(\mathbb{S})
            this way, see for instance (
            %a{:href => "#WangXu10"}> Wang-Xu 10, section 2
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Putnam"}> Putnam
            ).
          %p For instance
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 0
                        %mi fr
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{fr}_0 = \mathbb{Z}
                because there are two
                = succeed "-framings" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi O
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(O(k)) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_2
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                        %mi fr
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^{fr}_1 = \mathbb{Z}_2
                because the only compact connected 1-manifold is the circle, there are two framings on the circle, corresponding to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi O
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(O(k)) \simeq \mathbb{Z}_2
                and they are their own negatives.
          %h3#jhomomorphism_and_adams_einvariant J-homomorphism and Adams e-invariant
          %p
            The following characterizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J-homomorphism"} J-homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  J \;\colon\; \pi_\bullet(O) \longrightarrow \pi_\bullet(\mathbb{S})
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+orthogonal+group"} stable orthogonal group
            to the stable homotopy groups of spheres. This was first conjectured in (
            %a{:href => "#Adams66"}> Adams 66
            ) (since called the
            = succeed ")" do
              %em Adams conjecture
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Quillen71"} Quillen 71
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By the discussion at
              %em
                %a{:href => "orthogonal%20group#HomotopyGroups"} orthogonal group – homotopy groups
              we have that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+orthogonal+group"} stable orthogonal group
              are
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
